// srv3's HOST OS endpoint, absent from this topology until 2026-07-24 — only srv3_bmc_endpoint
// (192.168.1.192) was modeled, so the model could name srv3's out-of-band controller but not the
// machine itself. Observed live from the operator's DHCP table (192.168.1.221, MAC
// 48:21:0B:81:C7:1D) and confirmed addressable by probe (sshd answers on :22). REACHABLE via the
// srv2 hop (corrected 2026-07-25): ubuntu@srv3 runs commands today authenticated by srv2's local
// node-orch key (proven live — hostname=srv3, aarch64, 128 cores). What is NOT yet present is the
// FLEET identity: the fleet-automation-ssh-key is authorized for no user on srv3 (born Jul 13 with
// ssh.authorized-keys EMPTY, hand-keyed post-install with node-orch@srv2 / briansrls@srv1 / macbook
// only), the B2 runner-user + A1 secret-materialization gap in
// gunbc.plans.fleet_subsumption_manual_gaps — a one-time host_authorized_keys_reconcile upsert over
// the working node-orch hop, not a networking fact and not an unreachable host. LEASE STATUS: as of
// 2026-07-24 the operator converted this to a STATIC reservation (192.168.1.221 ↔ MAC
// 48:21:0B:81:C7:1D), so the literal is now a fact rather than a lease-lifetime observation —
// matching the srv1/srv2 hosts and the srv1/srv2/srv3 BMCs. STILL DYNAMIC and therefore
// provisional: srv4's host (192.168.1.196) and srv4's BMC (192.168.1.195); those two literals
// remain only as stable as their leases until the operator reserves them. The DHCP-reservation act
// itself is an unmodeled manual step for srv1..4 — a static-lease intent (MAC ↔ address binding on
// the operator's router) has no carrier for those hosts; see
// gunbc.plans.fleet_subsumption_manual_gaps for where that gap is tracked. PARTIAL CLOSURE
// (SPARK-LANE-A, 2026-08-07): gunbc.spark.dgx_procurement's DgxSparkRouterBinding is that carrier
// for srv5/srv6, holding the cited extdeps.router.verizon_cr1000a reservation row Optionally and
// refusing deployment admission while it is Absent — and the two reservations the operator authored
// for the DGX Spark units ARE modeled there as observed router rows, held apart from the slot
// bindings until the operator states which box serves which slot. It is deliberately NOT
// retrofitted to srv1..4 in the same change: those four hosts are already reserved and enrolled, so
// a binding row for them would be authored from the live router rather than ahead of arrival, and
// that is a read-transport question (the cr1000a lease-table transport is itself an open extdeps
// frontier row) rather than the pre-arrival intent question srv5/srv6 pose.

data srv3_host_lan_endpoint: NetworkEndpoint = NetworkEndpoint {
  label: "srv3-host-lan",
  subject: EndpointHost { host: operator_host_srv3 },
  address: "192.168.1.221",
  role: HostOperatingSystem,
  locality: Lan,
}

// DCH-0 (2026-09-02). THE TWO ROWS BELOW ARE THE ENROLMENT ACT, and this note says what they do and
// what they deliberately do not do, because in this module authoring the row IS the act rather than
// a description of one.
//
// ADDRESS PROVENANCE. Both literals are the wireless-keyed CR1000A static reservations transcribed in
// gunbc.spark.dgx_procurement -- srv5 holds spark_a3ee_reservation (192.168.1.226) and srv6 holds
// spark_3bd5_reservation (192.168.1.225), joined to these slots by
// dgx_spark_router_binding_operator_allocation, an attributable OperatorAllocation and not a
// measurement. THIS IS A SECOND SPELLING OF ONE ADDRESS AND IT IS ADMITTED RATHER THAN HIDDEN: the
// procurement module imports THIS one for operator_host_srv5/srv6, so a projection from the binding
// is refused by the import graph's one law -- measured by adding it and compiling, `circular
// dependency detected: gunbc.fleet_intent_network -> gunbc.spark.dgx_procurement`. The duplication
// therefore dissolves with the 🟡 dissolve-on note above it: when NetworkEndpoint.address re-grounds
// onto extdeps.network.address.IpAddress, these two rows converge onto the SAME Ipv4Address value the
// reservation rows already carry, exactly as srv2/srv3 converge onto gunbc.fleet_reach's rows. Until
// then the reservation is the authority and this is a transcription of it, marked as one.
//
// WHAT ENROLMENT HERE DOES NOT DO, stated because the adjacent act is the dangerous one. Membership in
// gunbc.fleet_intent's ComputeHost / fleet_intent_known_hosts list is a SEPARATE act with three
// production consumers, one of which -- gunbc.runner.runner_host_deploy `admit_runner_host` -- is a
// fail-closed enrolment wall that currently protects these two machines by returning
// RunnerHostUnenrolled. Nothing here touches that list, so that wall still stands for srv5/srv6.
//
// THE LIVE RECONCILIATION IS OUTSTANDING, AND IT IS ALREADY REPRESENTABLE RATHER THAN PROSE. No
// observed-vs-desired readback of the rendered serving unit has been performed on either host. That
// obligation needs no new carrier: gunbc.spark.serving_observation_scope derives its observation
// denominator from dgx_spark_reserved_identities(), which already names srv5 and srv6, so
// spark_observation_coverage answers SparkObservationIncomplete with both hosts in `missing` until a
// probe transaction supplies them. The readback needs host access this lane does not hold; the gap is
// therefore reported by that join and not asserted here.
data srv5_host_lan_endpoint: NetworkEndpoint = NetworkEndpoint {
  label: "srv5-host-lan",
  subject: EndpointHost { host: operator_host_srv5 },
  address: "192.168.1.226",
  role: HostOperatingSystem,
  locality: Lan,
}

